# The git hooks (193 B.1) are POSIX shell scripts, and git runs them with its
# own sh. With `core.autocrlf=true` (this repository's setting on Windows) a
# tracked shell script is written back to the working tree with CRLF, and the
# first line becomes `#!/bin/sh\r` — which git's sh reads as the interpreter
# `/bin/sh\r: not found`, so the hook stops guarding and says nothing. Pinning
# these paths to LF is what makes the hooks survive a fresh clone here.
/.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Contract artifacts are pinned to LF for the same reason the hooks are, with a
# second one: contracts/differential/manifest.json records SHA-256 hashes of the
# golden *bytes*, so a checkout that turns '\n' into '\r\n' would pin a different
# manifest per platform. Every contract JSON is generated through
# `tools.differential_compare.write_text_lf`, which writes '\n' explicitly.
/contracts/**/*.json text eol=lf

# The symbol book and the module catalog/manifest record SHA-256 of file *bytes*:
# `index.profileBookSha256` hashes profiles.json whole, and manifest.json pins the
# digest of every generated module file. With `core.autocrlf=true` (this repo's
# Windows setting) a fresh clone would write those files back as CRLF, so every
# recorded digest would stop describing the file on disk and the book checker
# would go red on a machine that changed nothing. Pinning LF is what makes a
# clone byte-identical to the checkout the digests were taken in. The module
# files are generated through the Rust writer, which emits LF explicitly.
#
# `**` spans zero directories as well as one or more (`/contracts/**/*.json`
# already relies on that for `contracts/differential/manifest.json`), so the two
# rules below also cover the module root files catalog.json and manifest.json —
# verified with `git check-attr`, not assumed. Deliberately not `*.json`: every
# other JSON in this repository is none of this contract's business.
/blocklib/symbols/*.json text eol=lf
/blocklib/modules/**/*.json text eol=lf
